;; Sweep tasks for karamazov-ylte.1, against endless-flight @ e4afef0.
;;
;; GRADED ON PURPOSE. The first live smoke run showed that a trivial task
;; produces ZERO supervisor edits — 42 turns, 55 factory userspace rows, none
;; project-authored — because oversight/worth-a-look? fires on unmet gates,
;; idle turns, crashes, the soft cap or nobody-shipping, and a task that ships
;; cleanly trips none of them. Task difficulty is therefore a design parameter
;; for ylte.7's signal in a way it is not for the outcome metrics, and these
;; three climb deliberately.
;;
;; EVERY DEFECT NAMED HERE IS VERIFIED, not inferred: shot.png at e4afef0 shows
;; the ground ending in a hard line against bare sky, trees standing past that
;; edge with nothing under them, and the bird and its rings riding far above
;; the canopy.

{:subject {:repo "/Users/yogthos/src/raylib-examples/endless-flight"
           :sha "e4afef0"
           :verify-cmd "jolt -M:test"
           :baseline-suite "63 tests, 1221 assertions, 0 failures"}

 ;; Appended to every task. This is the "make it complex enough" half: it is
 ;; what forces multi-file work, a real seam, and an end-to-end exercise rather
 ;; than a constant edit plus a green suite (karamazov-ksl).
 :standing-requirements
 "Hold to the project's existing discipline while you do this:

  - The pure/impure split is the point of this codebase. flight.terrain,
    flight.mechanics, flight.render and flight.game are pure and fully
    testable with no raylib and no Box3D; flight.draw is the only namespace
    that may call rl/. New logic goes on the pure side with tests, and
    flight.draw only calls it. A calculation that lands in flight.draw is
    untestable by construction and is the wrong answer.
  - Add tests for every pure function you add, in the matching test namespace,
    and add that namespace to test/flight/test_runner.clj if it is new.
  - The whole suite must be green when you are done: `jolt -M:test`, currently
    63 tests and 1221 assertions.
  - If a change makes an EXISTING test wrong, do not delete it and do not
    quietly weaken it. Decide what the invariant should now be, rewrite the
    test to pin THAT, and say in one line why the old one no longer holds.
  - Exercise it, do not just unit-test it. `jolt -M:run` with
    RAYLIB_APP_AUTO_QUIT_MS set writes a screenshot on flight.draw/shot-at-frame;
    scripts/png_hist.py reads a png's colour histogram. Use them to confirm the
    change is actually visible, and say what you saw.
  - Do not change the deps.edn paths or the native library wiring."

 :tasks
 ;; PER-TASK BUDGETS. max-turns is not only "how much work" — gates.edn keys
 ;; the steering off it (:wind-down-fraction 0.85, :last-call-window 2,
 ;; :turn-budget-notices [0.6 0.85]), so a cap that is tight for the task puts
 ;; the branch into wind-down and last-call while it is still working, and the
 ;; gate profile measures the cap instead of the loop. The smoke run showed the
 ;; degenerate form: max-turns 6 put last-call at turn 5 and it fired 12 times.
 ;; These are sized so wind-down lands after the work plausibly fits, and they
 ;; differ per task because the tasks differ in scope by construction. Runs are
 ;; compared WITHIN a task, never across, so unequal budgets cost nothing.
 [{:id :horizon-fade
   :difficulty :medium
   :max-turns 60
   :timeout-ms 2700000
   :provokes "no existing test to break; the hard part is inventing the pure
              seam, since draw_test.clj only smoke-tests that rl/ names resolve"
   :problem
   "The world visibly ends. flight.draw/draw-terrain! draws a
    (2*terrain-half+1) square of ground columns — terrain-half is 7 at
    terrain-step 14.0, so ground exists only within about 105 metres of the
    bird — and past that the sky starts in a hard horizontal line. Worse,
    draw-trees! uses tree-radius 190.0, so trees keep being drawn for another
    85 metres beyond where the ground stops and stand silhouetted against
    bare sky with nothing underneath them. shot.png in the repo shows both.

    Make the terrain fade out into the distance so a player never sees it end.
    Blend ground and trees toward the sky colour as they recede, so the far
    edge dissolves into the horizon instead of terminating at one, and make
    sure nothing is ever drawn standing past the ground it should be sitting
    on. Keep the near field looking exactly as it does now — this should read
    as depth, not as a haze over the whole scene."}

  {:id :low-course
   :difficulty :hard
   :max-turns 80
   :timeout-ms 3300000
   :provokes "collides head-on with ring_course_test/consecutive-rings-clear-terrain
              and ring-zero-above-its-own-ground, which pin
              (>= clearance game/ring-clearance). The agent must decide the
              invariant has CHANGED and re-pin it, not delete it."
   :problem
   "The flight is too safe and too high to be interesting. flight.game holds
    ring-clearance at 22.0 metres of air over the worst ground in the corridor,
    while flight.terrain/tree-height is 9.0 — so the ring course always floats
    well above the forest and the bird rides above it, as shot.png shows.

    Bring the whole thing down into the trees. Lower the bird's flight so it
    travels through the forest canopy rather than over it, and place the rings
    down among the trees so that threading one means flying between trunks.
    The course must still be flyable: a player who lines up on a ring and holds
    a reasonable line should be able to make it without an unavoidable
    collision, and the rings must still be reachable one after another. PLAN.md
    asks for exactly this — rings placed so they are reasonable to reach but
    carry a real chance of hitting an obstacle, so it is not too easy.

    Two tests in test/flight/ring_course_test.clj currently assert the old
    clearance. They are asserting the behaviour you are deliberately changing;
    work out what the course must now guarantee instead and pin that."}

  {:id :fast-low-endless
   :difficulty :hardest
   :max-turns 110
   :timeout-ms 4200000
   :provokes "three coupled changes that interact — speed shortens the reaction
              window that the lowered course just narrowed, and both make the
              draw-distance edge more visible per second of flight. Touches
              mechanics, game, terrain, render and draw."
   :problem
   "Three changes to the feel of the flight, and they interact — do them
    together and reconcile them, do not treat them as three separate edits.

    1. Speed it up. flight.mechanics currently flies at base-speed 16.0 with
       max-speed 46.0 and boost-speed 40.0. The flight should feel considerably
       faster than it does now.

    2. Drop into the trees. Lower the bird's flight so it travels through the
       forest rather than above it, and put the rings down among the trees so
       threading one means flying between trunks — see ring-clearance 22.0 in
       flight.game against tree-height 9.0 in flight.terrain. The course must
       stay flyable: reachable ring to ring, with a real chance of hitting
       something, which is what PLAN.md asks for.

    3. Make the horizon endless. Ground is only drawn within about 105 metres
       (terrain-half 7 at terrain-step 14.0) and ends in a hard line against
       the sky, while trees are drawn out to 190 metres and stand past that
       edge with nothing under them. Fade the distance into the sky so the
       player never sees the world end.

    Now reconcile them. Flying faster through a lower, tighter course shortens
    the time a player has to react to each ring, and it eats the visible ground
    ahead faster than it did before — a draw distance that was adequate at
    16 units per second may not be at the new speed. Judge whether ring
    spacing, draw distance or the camera need to move with the speed, decide,
    and say what you decided and why.

    Tests in test/flight/ring_course_test.clj assert the clearance you are
    changing. Re-pin them to what the course must now guarantee."}]}
